PLACES=[
# ---- Claybank ----
N('claybank-brick-plant','Claybank Brick Plant','Claybank','sk','industry','standing',50.03062,-105.21985,'site','public','walk','','1912–1989',
  'A brick works at the foot of the Dirt Hills that made firebrick and face brick from clay dug on the spot, from 1914 to 1989. Its refractory brick went to railways, oil refineries, power plants and smelters, and its face brick to buildings across the prairies; Wikipedia names the Bessborough in Saskatoon, the Château Frontenac and the Gravelbourg cathedral. It became a national historic site in 1994 (the register; Wikipedia says 1996) as an unusually complete early-20th-century brick plant.',
  'The register lists the whole works of 1912 to 1937 still in place: ten round domed brick kilns in two rows with five stacks between them, the attached factory buildings (clay shed, engine room, tunnel dryers, hand mould shop), the brick office with OFFICE spelled in brick over the door, a brick bunkhouse, cottages, stock sheds, the narrow-gauge rail line, and machinery in place (clay grinder, dry pans, brick press, line shafts and belts). The clay pits are in the hills behind, with trails through the Massold Clay Canyons. The National Trust put it on its 2025 endangered list: the buildings are slipping into disrepair and are kept up mostly by volunteers.',
  'The brick plant that built the Bessborough, still with its ten kilns.',
  'Run as a museum by its own society (claybankbrick.ca), on Highway 339 about 15 km west of Avonlea (Museums Association listing). For 2026 the site gave daily 9 a.m. to 5 p.m., with the season closing September 7; the Museums Association listing gives July and August and asks visitors to call ahead. Admission there: adults $10, youth $5, seniors $6, family $25. Café on site. No parking is mapped; where to park was not checked.',
  [W('Claybank Brick Plant'),HP('Claybank Brick Plant National Historic Site of Canada',9350),L('Claybank Brick Plant (official site)','https://www.claybankbrick.ca'),L('Museums Association of Saskatchewan: Claybank Brick Plant','https://saskmuseums.org/?p=1160'),L('Canada\'s History / National Trust: Claybank endangered','https://canadashistory.ca/explore/historic-sites/claybank-brick-plant-national-historic-site')],conf='mixed',brick='no'),
# ---- Abernethy ----
N('motherwell-homestead','Motherwell Homestead (Lanark Place)','Abernethy','sk','town','standing',50.71915,-103.42419,'exact','public','walk','','1882–1939',
  'W. R. Motherwell, from Ontario, homesteaded here from 1882 and laid out a model farm on scientific principles: the yard split into four quarters (house, farmyard, garden, water) behind planted shelter belts. He became Saskatchewan\'s first minister of agriculture and federal minister of agriculture in the 1920s. Parks Canada keeps the farmstead restored to 1907.',
  'A two-storey Italianate fieldstone house of 1897 with bracketed eaves, iron cresting and a two-storey front porch; inside, wood wainscotting, a pressed-metal ceiling in the hall, plaster rosettes and a wood mantel (register). The L-shaped gambrel barn (1896–1907) stands on a one-storey wall of split granite fieldstone with board-and-batten above and three cupolas. Also a hired men\'s cottage, outbuildings, fences and shelter belts. The buildings are open to visitors in season, with staff in period dress (Wikipedia, Parks Canada).',
  'The model prairie farm of the man who ran Canada\'s farm policy.',
  'Parks Canada national historic site, 3 km south of Abernethy: past the village continue on Kenlis Road (gravel) for 2 km (Parks Canada). Open seasonally May to September, with a café; it was closed for the season when looked at in November 2026. Exact hours and fees not checked (the hours page is drawn by script).',
  [W('Motherwell Homestead'),HP('Motherwell Homestead National Historic Site of Canada',1209),HP('Motherwell House',3342),HP('Motherwell Homestead Barn',3341),L('Parks Canada: Plan your visit','https://www.parks.canada.ca/lhn-nhs/sk/motherwell/visit')],th=[50.71735,-103.42495],thn='Lot by the visitor centre (OpenStreetMap)',brick='no'),
N('christ-anglican-abernethy','Christ Anglican Church, Abernethy','Abernethy','sk','civic','standing',50.74744,-103.41939,'exact','view','drive','','1886',
  'A small wood-frame Gothic country church built in 1886 about 2 km south of Abernethy and moved into the village in 1904. The register calls it the oldest church in the Abernethy area and the oldest building in the village; it is still used for worship.',
  'Steep gable roof and pointed-arch window frames on a plain vernacular plan (register). Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'A working Anglican church on Burton Street in the village (OpenStreetMap); see it from the street. Service times not checked.',
  [HP('Christ Anglican Church',1981)],brick='no'),
# ---- Fort Qu'Appelle, Lebret, Katepwa ----
N('fort-quappelle-hbc','Fort Qu\'Appelle: the Hudson\'s Bay Company post building (Fort Qu\'Appelle Museum)','Fort Qu\'Appelle','sk','fort','standing',50.77112,-103.79834,'exact','public','drive','','1864',
  'The Hudson\'s Bay Company opened a provision post here in 1864, a small log-palisaded fort at the centre of a web of prairie trails; it closed about eight years later as the bison disappeared, and the town grew from it. Treaty 4 was negotiated at the fort in 1874, and Middleton\'s militia camped here in 1885. One building of the post survives, now the core of the town museum.',
  'The one original post building, which the Museums Association listing calls part of the Chief Factor\'s house, joined to two later additions, on a grassy lot at the north edge of town with a federal plaque (register). Displays on First Nations, the North West Mounted Police and Fort San. The museum also opens Archibald McDonald House, the 1886 home of the post\'s last Chief Factor (Wikipedia; the listing gives it a separate $10 fee). Interior fabric of the post building not checked.',
  'The last building of the fort where Treaty 4 was made.',
  'Fort Qu\'Appelle Heritage Museum, 198 Bay Avenue North. The Museums Association listing gives May 15 to September 15, Sunday to Friday 1 to 5 p.m. and Saturday 9 a.m. to 5 p.m.; admission $5 a person, $10 a family; by email outside those hours. Street parking nearby; a town lot is on 3rd Street West.',
  [W('Fort Qu\'Appelle'),HP('Fort Qu\'Appelle National Historic Site of Canada',18988),L('Museums Association of Saskatchewan: Fort Qu\'Appelle Heritage Museum','https://saskmuseums.org/places/fort-quappelle-museum/')],brick='?'),
N('fort-quappelle-gtp-station','Grand Trunk Pacific station, Fort Qu\'Appelle','Fort Qu\'Appelle','sk','rail','standing',50.76385,-103.79456,'exact','view','drive','stations','1911–1962',
  'A second-class station built by the Grand Trunk Pacific in 1911 to its standard plan "A", which marked Fort Qu\'Appelle as the distribution centre of the district. The Canadian National took the line over in 1919 and the station served passengers and freight until 1962. It still stands on its first site beside Highway 10.',
  'A storey-and-a-half wood-frame building with stucco below, shingles on the upper floor, a bell-cast hipped roof with dormers and deep eaves, bay windows and one large open room on the main floor (register). The register mentions use as a tourist information booth and meeting room; its present use and whether it is open were not checked.',
  '',
  'On the railway right-of-way beside Highway 10 in town; see it from the road. Owner and opening not checked.',
  [W('Fort Qu\'Appelle'),HP('Grand Trunk Pacific Railway Station',4884)],brick='no'),
N('lebret-sacred-heart','Sacred Heart Church and the Stations of the Cross, Lebret','Lebret','sk','civic','standing',50.75664,-103.70777,'exact','view','walk','','1865–1929',
  'Bishop Taché founded a Catholic mission here in 1865 (the Catholic Register; the register says 1865, Wikipedia 1866), the oldest Catholic parish in southern Saskatchewan and once the centre of a residential school and an Oblate training house. The present fieldstone church was begun in 1925 and dedicated on July 1, 1926; it marked its centenary with a mass in August 2026. The first pilgrimage chapel on the hill was burned in 1928; the present chapel and the fourteen stations up the hill date from 1929.',
  'A fieldstone church in the Quebec manner: metal-clad belfry with a pediment on columns, round-arched traceried windows and Tyndall stone trim on the front (register). North of the village, across the highway, a gate marks where the first cross stood (1865) and a path climbs past fourteen large white crosses to the small wood chapel on the hilltop. The 1886 rectory was demolished in 2005. Interior not checked.',
  'Fourteen white crosses climbing a valley hill to a chapel.',
  'A working Roman Catholic parish church at the head of the main street; the stations are on a separate parish parcel on the hill. Service times and whether the church is open between services were not checked. No mapped parking.',
  [W('Lebret, Saskatchewan'),HP('Sacred Heart Parish, Rectory and The Stations of the Cross',6393),L('Catholic Register: Sacred Heart wasn\'t always a cottage country church','https://catholicregister.org/item/20372-sacred-heart-wasn-t-always-a-cottage-country-church'),L('Grasslands News: Lebret church celebrates 100th anniversary (2026)','https://grasslandsnews.ca/2026/08/28/lebret-church-celebrates-100th-anniversary/')],conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('all-saints-katepwa','All Saints Anglican Church, Katepwa Beach','Katepwa Beach','sk','civic','standing',50.6693,-103.601,'area','view','drive','','1887',
  'A wood-frame church built by settlers in 1887, the oldest church in the area by its register entry, used for services and community gatherings for most of the years since.',
  'Gothic Revival touches: tall narrow pointed-arch windows with shutters. The church stands back from the road in a churchyard with a cemetery, inside a fence and hedge (register). Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'In Pelletier Park, Resort Village of Katepwa Beach (register). See it from the road; services and opening not checked. The pin marks the area; the church was not picked out on the aerial.',
  [HP('All Saints Anglican Church',1644)],brick='no'),
# ---- Qu'Appelle ----
N('st-peters-quappelle','St. Peter\'s Anglican Church (first Pro-Cathedral of Qu\'Appelle)','Qu\'Appelle','sk','civic','standing',50.54046,-103.87937,'site','view','drive','','1885',
  'A brick church consecrated on June 30, 1885. When St. Paul\'s in Regina declined to become the cathedral, Bishop Anson made the Town of Qu\'Appelle the seat of the new Diocese of Qu\'Appelle and St. Peter\'s its pro-cathedral, which it remained until 1944. The parish hall beside it dates from 1905.',
  'The register lists the furnishings sent for a cathedral: a banner of St. Peter embroidered by nuns in the south of France, an oak lectern of 1886, a bishop\'s chair paid for from England, and a carved oak reredos made in Oberammergau. Whether visitors can see the interior outside services was not checked.',
  'A prairie town\'s cathedral, with a reredos carved in Oberammergau.',
  '37 Walsh Street, a working Anglican church; see it from the street. Service times not checked.',
  [HP('St. Peter\'s Anglican Church',4895)],brick='?'),
N('quappelle-town-hall','Qu\'Appelle Town Hall and opera hall','Qu\'Appelle','sk','civic','standing',50.54097,-103.87944,'site','view','drive','','1906',
  'Built in 1906, when Qu\'Appelle was a busy trading town, to hold the council chamber and courtroom, fire hall, police quarters, a basement jail and an opera hall upstairs. The fire brigade left in 1959 and the constable in 1968; it is still the town office. The Grand Trunk Pacific bypassed the town in 1911 and its growth stopped.',
  'A square two-storey Romanesque Revival hall in buff brick on a fieldstone base, by William Dodd: crenellated tower, corbelled cornices, TOWN HALL lettered over the door, and two big round-headed doorways, the old fire-hall one still reached by its earthen ramp. Inside, the register lists the double-height opera hall with a 1921 tin ceiling, the two-cell basement jail and a maple staircase. Interior access not checked.',
  '',
  '25 9th Avenue (register); the town office. See it from the street; whether the hall can be visited was not checked.',
  [HP('Qu\'Appelle Town Hall',13542)],brick='no'),
# ---- Wolseley ----
N('wolseley-court-house','Wolseley Court House','Wolseley','sk','civic','standing',50.42634,-103.27035,'exact','view','walk','','1893–1909',
  'Built in 1893–1895 to plans from Thomas Fuller\'s federal architect\'s office, the only court house left from the Territorial days and the oldest in Saskatchewan (register). It stopped hearing cases in 1909 and has since been a Mounted Police jail, a boys\' detention home and part of a nursing home (Town of Wolseley; the town gives 1894 as the year built).',
  'A one-storey brick building on a fieldstone foundation with a hipped roof, symmetrical windows, and the porch, cupola and sheet-metal roof put back in the early 1980s (register). It sits on a landscaped lot above Fairly Lake; a pedestrian swinging bridge (rebuilt in 2004 on a crossing first bridged in 1905) links it to the main street. The town adopted a civic re-use plan for it in 2014. Interior not checked.',
  'Saskatchewan\'s oldest court house, reached by a swinging bridge.',
  'Provincial heritage property at the southeast corner of Ouimet and Richmond Streets; see it from the street. The swinging bridge and the lake paths are open public ways (Town of Wolseley). Whether the building is open was not checked.',
  [HP('Wolseley Court House',2778),L('Town of Wolseley: Visitor information','https://wolseley.ca/?p=450'),L('Tourism Saskatchewan: Wolseley Heritage Tour & Swinging Bridge','https://www.tourismsaskatchewan.com/listings/190/wolseley-heritage-tour--swinging-bridge')],conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('wolseley-opera-house','Wolseley Town Hall / Opera House','Wolseley','sk','civic','standing',50.42428,-103.27121,'exact','view','walk','','1906–1907',
  'A brick and fieldstone town hall of 1906–07 that held the town office, fire hall, library, jail cell and an auditorium for travelling shows and later films. Structural problems closed it in 1989; it was restored in the early 1990s and is used again as the town\'s hall. The register counts it among nineteen such town hall/opera houses once built in Saskatchewan.',
  'By Manitoba architect J. H. G. Russell, built like a Baroque church: tall proportions, steep roof, scroll buttresses and a big central bell tower. The hall is on the main floor over the raised-basement office, with the fire hall at the back; the FIRE HALL sign is still over the east door (Town of Wolseley). Tours of the inside by appointment (Tourism Saskatchewan).',
  '',
  'Corner of Richmond and Varennes Streets (the register gives 510 Varennes Street; OpenStreetMap 610). Owned by the town; see it from the street, inside by appointment through the town. Pick up the town\'s heritage tour booklet in town.',
  [HP('Wolseley Town Hall/Opera House',2378),L('Town of Wolseley: Visitor information','https://wolseley.ca/?p=450'),L('Tourism Saskatchewan: Wolseley Heritage Tour & Swinging Bridge','https://www.tourismsaskatchewan.com/listings/190/wolseley-heritage-tour--swinging-bridge')],brick='?'),
# ---- Moose Mountain ----
N('cannington-manor','Cannington Manor: village site and All Saints log church','Cannington Manor','sk','town','ruins',49.7337,-102.04596,'site','public','walk','','1882–1902',
  'Captain Edward Pierce founded Cannington Manor in 1882 as a colony of English gentlemen farmers, with a farming college for remittance men, horse racing, polo, fox hunts and plays. A store, flour mill, hotel, dairy and land titles office followed, for about 200 people. Pierce died in 1888, and when the railway was laid about 10 to 16 km south in 1901–02 the village emptied. It has been a provincial historic park since 1986.',
  'A 15-hectare parcel with cellars, walls and foundations, marked sites of vanished buildings along the old main street, the flour mill foundation, replica log and frame buildings and a 1920s schoolhouse as the visitor centre (register). All Saints, a shiplap-covered log church of 1884 (Wikipedia and the register; the Encyclopedia of Saskatchewan gives 1885) with its cemetery, stands in the middle of the village but is not park property. Three km west, a second park parcel holds the 1888 Humphrys/Hewlett House, with its two-storey hall, grand staircase and darkroom, and the broken stone walls of two outbuildings.',
  'Polo, fox hunts and a gentleman\'s college on the prairie, and what is left of it.',
  'Sask Parks provincial historic park, 26 km southeast of Moose Mountain Provincial Park or 16 km northeast of Manor; the four roads in are gravel grid roads (Sask Parks brochure). The park office is the Moose Mountain office, 306-577-2600. A 2021 news report gave Thursday to Monday, 10 a.m. to 5 p.m. in summer; current hours and fees not checked (the park\'s page is drawn by script). The Sask Parks driving tour warns that several stops on the roads around (Rosemont and others) are private homes: view them from the road only.',
  [W('Cannington Manor Provincial Park'),HP('Cannington Manor Provincial Park',4167),L('Sask Parks: Cannington Manor driving tour (PDF)','https://tsaskblobstorage.blob.core.windows.net/ics-docs/Cannington_Manor_Driving_Tour_FINAL.pdf'),L('Encyclopedia of Saskatchewan: Cannington Manor','https://esask.uregina.ca/entry/cannington_manor.html'),L('SaskToday: Cannington Manor is open for the summer (2021)','https://www.sasktoday.ca/south/local-news/cannington-manor-is-open-for-the-summer-4172950')],conf='mixed',brick='no'),
# ---- Lower Qu'Appelle ----
N('fort-esperance','Fort Espérance (North West Company post site)','Rocanville','sk','fort','traces',50.4933,-101.599,'area','public','walk','','1785–1824',
  'Robert Grant built Fort Espérance for the North West Company in 1785–87 as its chief post on the Great Plains and its main pemmican depot, feeding the canoe brigades to the Churchill and Athabasca. It was abandoned in 1810, rebuilt on a nearby knoll in 1816, replaced by a post farther east in 1819, held by the Hudson\'s Bay Company from 1821 and closed in 1824 (register; Wikipedia gives 1787 to 1819). A national historic site since 1944 (Wikipedia) or 1959 (register).',
  'Parks Canada says to allow about an hour to see the remains of a cellar and fireplace and to walk the hollows where both forts stood, on the south bank of the Qu\'Appelle and a knoll nearby. Tourism Saskatchewan says the site is marked by a cairn, with a picnic area.',
  'The pemmican depot that fed the fur brigades to the Athabasca.',
  'Parks Canada national historic site off Highway 600 between Spy Hill and Rocanville: 18 km southeast of Spy Hill, then 1.4 km west (Tourism Saskatchewan). Limited day-use facilities. Hours and fees not checked. The pin is the register point and marks the general area, not the cellar.',
  [W('Fort Espérance'),HP('Fort Espérance National Historic Site of Canada',11713),L('Parks Canada: Fort Espérance, plan your visit','https://parks.canada.ca/lhn-nhs/sk/esperance/visit'),L('Tourism Saskatchewan: Fort Espérance','https://www.tourismsaskatchewan.com/listings/962/fort-esprance-national-historic-site')],conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('esterhazy-flour-mill','Esterhazy Flour Mill','Esterhazy','sk','industry','standing',50.65254,-102.08026,'exact','view','drive','','1906–1907',
  'A heavy-timber flour mill with a wood-crib grain elevator joined to it, built in 1906–07 by J. Sanders, who owned mills in Manitoba and Saskatchewan. The register calls it the oldest and most complete timber flour mill in Saskatchewan; it is a provincial heritage property (2005) and a national historic site (2007). The town re-sided it in 2021.',
  'Most of the original milling machinery and fittings are inside, with hardwood floors in the milling area, post-and-beam framing with pegged joints, hand-made chutes, and a 1947 diesel engine that replaced steam in the late 1940s or early 1950s. The elevator has the early offset cupola (register, Town of Esterhazy). Also a wood office, sheds and an engine room.',
  'A timber flour mill with its machinery still in it.',
  'Owned by the Town of Esterhazy, 517 Smith-Dorrien Street. Tours by arrangement: the visitor information centre, 306-745-5406, May to August, or the town office, 306-745-3942, September to April (Town of Esterhazy). See it from the street otherwise.',
  [HP('Esterhazy Flour Mill',10427),L('Town of Esterhazy: Flour Mill','https://townofesterhazy.ca/p/flour-mill')],brick='no'),
# ---- Estevan ----
N('wood-end-nwmp-post','Wood End North West Mounted Police post','Estevan','sk','fort','standing',49.13187,-102.97272,'site','public','drive','','1883–1897',
  'A two-storey wood building put up in 1883 when the Mounted Police reached southeast Saskatchewan, one of a string of border outposts; its first site had been used by the International Boundary Commission in 1873–74 and was named for the end of firewood to the west. It closed in 1897 and was later moved into Estevan. The register calls it the oldest Mounted Police post left in Saskatchewan.',
  'A plain Georgian Revival wood building with a single-pitched roof, symmetrical front and wood trim round the windows and doors (register). It holds the seasonal museum of the Estevan Art Gallery & Museum, with its North West Mounted Police and 1874 March West collections. It is not on its original site.',
  '',
  'Estevan Art Gallery & Museum, 118 4th Street. Seasonal museum June to August, Monday to Friday 10 a.m. to 6 p.m., closed noon to 1 p.m. (Museums Association listing). Fees not checked. Street parking.',
  [HP('Wood End North West Mounted Police Post',2681),L('Museums Association of Saskatchewan: Estevan Art Gallery & Museum','https://saskmuseums.org/?p=1204')],brick='no'),
]
